## Reflection

The most important theme across these exercises was trust.

A switch learns source MAC addresses from the frames it receives. A DNS client trusts its configured resolver to provide the correct IP address. When those sources of information are not properly controlled or verified, an attacker may be able to manipulate the network’s behavior.

The defensive lesson is not simply to memorize individual attacks. It is to identify where a system accepts information based on trust and ask what controls verify that information.

Port security limits which MAC addresses may appear on a switch port. Trusted DNS resolvers limit who may answer DNS requests. DNSSEC provides cryptographic validation for supported records. TLS helps verify the identity of the destination after name resolution occurs.

Understanding these trust relationships helps connect the offensive technique to the defensive control designed to stop it.

